    "cons": [
      "No USB output, so no phone charging",
      "75 lumens maximum, about 6 hours on high"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheap, light lantern to buy in multiples: one sunny day refills a night of low light.",
    "description": "The Luci Original is the lantern that started the inflatable category, now sold under the BioLite name. It is simple: 10 cool white LEDs rated at up to 75 lumens, a 1,000 mAh (3.7 Wh) battery, and a panel BioLite rates to refill it in 7 hours of direct sun or 3 hours over USB-C. Runtime is listed at about 24 hours on low and 6 on high. That 7-hour solar figure is the key: one good day of sun on a windowsill or patio replaces a full night of low light, so a Luci Original can stay in balance with the sun indefinitely as a hallway, bathroom or tent light. It weighs 4.4 ounces and is IP67 rated. There is no USB output, so it cannot charge a phone, and 75 lumens is not enough to light a whole room. Buy three or four, not one."
  },
  {
    "id": "goal-zero-crush-light-chroma",
    "name": "Goal Zero Crush Light Chroma Collapsible Solar Lantern",
    "category": "Collapsible, 1.85 Wh, 60 Lumens, Color Modes",
    "badge": "Best Ultralight",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "3.2 ounces and collapses to about half an inch",
      "Nothing to inflate or puncture",
      "Six colors, a fade mode and a candle flicker mode",
      "USB recharge in 2.5 hours"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tiny 1.85 Wh battery: 3 hours on high, 35 on low",
      "20 hours of sun to refill from the built-in panel",
      "No phone charging"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A tent and backpack light first; too small to be anyone's main outage lantern.",
    "description": "The Crush Light is Goal Zero's collapsible solar lantern, and the Chroma version adds color. It folds like an accordion to just over half an inch, weighs 3.2 ounces and has no air bladder or valve to fail. Goal Zero rates it at 60 lumens on high for 3 hours, 25 lumens for 6 hours and 6 lumens for 35 hours, plus a candle mode at about 3.5 hours, from a 1.85 Wh (500 mAh) lithium battery. The built-in panel is rated at 20 hours to a full charge, which is slow for such a small battery, while USB takes about 2.5 hours. The color modes, six colors and a fade, make it a fun campsite or kids' tent light. As an outage lantern it is too small to light a room for long, and it cannot charge a phone, but as a pack-anywhere light that will never need batteries it does the job."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Solar Lantern",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-solar-lantern/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a solar lantern take to charge in the sun?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on the panel and battery. BioLite rates the Luci Original at 7 hours of direct sun, the Luci Charge 150 at about 14 and the Luci Charge 360 at about 28. LuminAID lists 16 to 20 hours for the Max and Goal Zero lists 20 for the Crush Light. Those are hours of direct sun, so expect one to six clear days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are solar lanterns good for power outages?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, especially for outages that last more than a day or two. Start them full from USB and they behave like any rechargeable lantern; after that the panel keeps a low or medium setting going indefinitely in decent weather. They do not replace a bright lantern for a whole room, but several around the house work well."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a solar lantern charge a phone?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some can. The LuminAID Max and Survivor and the BioLite Luci Charge 150 and 360 have USB output. A 2,000 mAh lantern adds roughly a third to half of a modern phone charge; the 6,000 mAh LuminAID Survivor is the one large enough to fully charge a typical phone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the brightest solar lantern?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On this list, the LuminAID Survivor at a rated 600 lumens, followed by the BioLite Luci Charge 360 at 360 lumens. Both are rated for only a few hours at full brightness, so most owners run them lower. For more sustained brightness, a large rechargeable lantern charged from a power station is the better tool."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do solar lanterns work on cloudy days?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They charge, but slowly. Cloud cover can cut a panel's output to a fraction of what it produces in direct sun, so a lantern that refills in two clear days may take a week of gray weather. That is why it pays to start every lantern full from USB and use low settings when the forecast is poor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do solar lantern batteries last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The built-in lithium batteries in these lanterns are not user-replaceable, and they gradually lose capacity with age and charge cycles. Several years of normal use is a reasonable expectation. Storing them partly charged at room temperature, and not leaving them baking in a hot car, helps them last longer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Inflatable or rigid solar lantern: which is better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For packing and emergency kits, inflatable. They fold flat, float, are rated waterproof and are light enough to keep several. Rigid lanterns stand up on their own and have no valve to fail, but most rigid solar models we found do not publish verifiable battery or charge-time specs, so we would buy a rigid rechargeable lantern instead."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Generators & Backup Power",
    tags: [
      "solar lantern",
      "solar camping lantern",
      "solar powered lantern",
      "solar lantern for power outage",
      "rechargeable solar lantern",
      "inflatable solar lantern"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Solar Lanterns</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A solar lantern is only as good as the sun it gets. Here are the six worth buying, ranked by what their panels can actually refill in a day, and which ones can keep a phone alive too.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#luminaid-max-2-in-1"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What a Solar Lantern Is</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The 6 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#sun-hours" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Panel Size vs Sun Hours</a>
          <a href="#usb-backup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. USB Charging Backup</a>
          <a href="#lumens-runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Lumens vs Runtime</a>
          <a href="#inflatable-vs-rigid" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Inflatable vs Rigid</a>
          <a href="#phone-charging" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Phone Charging Output</a>
          <a href="#power-outage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. For Power Outages</a>
          <a href="#camping" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. For Camping</a>
          <a href="#storage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Storage and Lifespan</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Every solar lantern box shows a sunny campsite and a full battery. The number that decides whether one is useful is printed much smaller: how many hours of direct sun the panel needs to refill the battery. On most of these lanterns that is between 7 and 28 hours, which is one to six clear days. Buy with that number in front of you and a solar lantern is the cheapest light you will never have to buy batteries for.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Solar Lantern Is, and What It Is Not
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Light and a Panel in One Body</h3>

          <p>
            A solar lantern is a small LED light with a lithium battery and a solar cell built into the same body. Leave it in the sun during the day and it lights a room, a tent or a picnic table at night. That is the whole idea, and it works, but only within the limits of a panel that is roughly the size of a coaster. None of the lanterns on this list carry a panel bigger than the face of the lantern itself, which caps how much energy they can collect in a day no matter how good the electronics are.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How It Differs From Battery and Rechargeable Lanterns</h3>

          <p>
            That makes a solar lantern a different tool from the two products it is usually compared with. A <a href="/best-battery-powered-lantern/">battery powered lantern</a> runs off AA or D cells or a big internal pack you charge from the wall, and it can be much brighter for much longer, but it stops when the batteries run out. A <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank</a> or a folding panel is built to move energy into a phone, and it has more panel area per dollar. The solar lantern sits between them: modest light, a modest battery, and a refill that does not need an outlet or a store.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Self-Refill Matters</h3>

          <p>
            The reason to own one is the refill. In an outage that lasts a week, or on a trip where there is no outlet for days, a lantern that tops itself up on a windowsill is a light that never quite runs out. In a one-night outage it is just a rechargeable lantern, and any decent rechargeable lantern will do. Be honest about which of those situations you are actually buying for, because it decides whether you need a big battery, a fast panel or just something small and cheap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Most Good Ones Now Fold Flat</h3>

          <p>
            Most of the good solar lanterns are now inflatable or collapsible. LuminAID and BioLite's Luci range fold flat to about an inch and puff up into a diffused cube or cylinder; Goal Zero's Crush Light collapses like an accordion. The flat shape is not just for packing. It is also what gives the panel its full face to the sun, which is why the rigid jar-style lanterns with a small cell on the lid tend to charge so slowly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 6 Best Solar Lanterns, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We compared the solar lanterns from LuminAID, BioLite (which now makes the Luci line that MPOWERD started) and Goal Zero on published lumens, battery capacity in watt-hours, the manufacturer's solar and USB recharge times, runtime on low and high, phone-charging output, water rating and packed weight. Every figure below is the manufacturer's rating or a retailer spec sheet; the sun-days and phone-charge numbers are arithmetic from those figures, not our own measurements. We left out generic rigid solar lanterns whose listings do not publish a battery capacity or a solar charge time we could check.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
