        <div id="sun-hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Panel Size vs Hours of Sun to a Full Charge
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rated in Hours of Direct Sun</h3>

          <p>
            The single most useful number on a solar lantern spec sheet is the solar recharge time, and it is almost always given in hours of direct sun, not hours of daylight. Those are very different things. A clear summer day in most of the US delivers something like five to six hours of strong, near-overhead sun; a clear winter day, or a day with high cloud, may deliver two to four. A lantern rated for 14 hours of sun to full is therefore a three-day refill in good weather and closer to a week in a gray December.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Size Against Solar Time</h3>

          <p>
            Divide each lantern's battery size by its rated solar time and a pattern appears. BioLite rates the Luci Original at 3.7 Wh in 7 hours, the Luci Charge 150 at 7.4 Wh in about 14 hours and the Luci Charge 360 at 14.8 Wh in about 28 hours. That is the same rate every time, roughly half a watt-hour per hour of sun, which tells you the panels are about the same size and the bigger lanterns simply have bigger tanks to fill. LuminAID's Max works out a little slower, at 7.4 Wh in 16 to 20 hours. Goal Zero's Crush Light is the outlier: 1.85 Wh in 20 hours, a deliberately tiny cell on a very light lantern.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
